5 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566136 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566136 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566136 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566136 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "487ea996-8321-5872-9dc6-dad9047db3c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566147 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566147 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566147 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566147 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186565913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566147 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566147 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566147 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566147 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566147 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "689f8fa2-4c6d-55d5-ace6-b3784c1ca904" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566203 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566203 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566203 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566203 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{8}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566203 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fd51843-9d70-54e0-8843-df58c7a06dd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566203 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{8} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{8} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4z-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $xz$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566234 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566234 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566234 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566234 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+8=\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566234 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "606d371f-fe47-59ac-b0e1-18679ee51770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566234 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+8 & =\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6} \\\\ 8 & =2+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6} & =6 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =36 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $y=-7,5.$ \n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-7,0)$ and $(0,5,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
